package = rawexec
---
// A raw `execv(2)` declaration — deliberately NOT via `platform.posix`, whose `execv` binding maps
// `const char *path` to a convenience Kotlin `String?` parameter. That convenience costs an implicit,
// malloc-backed interop memory scope at every call site to marshal the Kotlin String into a C string.
// `path` is typed `const void *` here (not `const char *`) purely to steer cinterop away from that
// same String convenience mapping — verified empirically: cinterop maps `const char *` to `String?`
// on ANY .def, not just platform.posix's, but leaves `const void *` (and pointer-array params, like
// `argv` below) as a raw pointer type. ProcessRunner.linux.kt calls this between `fork()` and the
// exec so the forked child performs provably zero allocation before replacing its image — a raw
// pointer built in the parent, pre-fork, and handed straight through.
int execv(const void *path, char *const argv[]);
